const CORE_PART1_QUESTIONS: Part1Question[] = [
  { id: "p1-01", topic: "Home", question: "Do you live in a house or an apartment?", answer: "I live in a medium-sized apartment near the city centre. It's not super fancy or anything, but it gets tons of natural light, and there's a little balcony where I can chill. The best part is that everything I need is within walking distance.", phrases: ["medium-sized apartment", "gets plenty of natural light", "within walking distance"] },
  { id: "p1-02", topic: "Hometown", question: "What do you like most about your hometown?", answer: "What I like most is the balance between convenience and a slower pace of life. You've got modern malls and good public transport, but you can still find quiet streets and local food markets. It feels lively without being too overwhelming.", phrases: ["a slower pace of life", "good public transport", "without being overwhelming"] },
  { id: "p1-03", topic: "Work or study", question: "What are you studying at the moment?", answer: "Right now I'm mainly studying English, with a real focus on the IELTS exam. I spend most of my time on speaking fluency and academic vocabulary. I enjoy it because I can see small but real improvements every week.", phrases: ["with a particular focus on", "speaking fluency", "meaningful improvements"] },
  { id: "p1-04", topic: "Mornings", question: "What is your morning routine like?", answer: "My mornings are pretty simple. I usually drink a glass of water, grab a quick breakfast, and check my plan for the day. If I've got time, I'll also listen to a short English podcast, 'cause it helps me switch into study mode.", phrases: ["fairly simple", "review my plan", "switch into study mode"] },
  { id: "p1-05", topic: "Weekends", question: "What do you usually do at weekends?", answer: "I try to keep my weekends balanced. Usually I finish a few personal errands in the morning, then meet friends or go for a long walk in the afternoon. I also leave a bit of time on Sunday evening to get ready for the week ahead.", phrases: ["keep my weekends balanced", "personal tasks", "the coming week"] },
  { id: "p1-06", topic: "Reading", question: "Do you enjoy reading books?", answer: "Yeah, especially non-fiction about psychology and personal growth. I like picking up ideas I can actually use in real life. I don't always read for long, but even ten pages before bed helps me slow down and think more clearly.", phrases: ["non-fiction books", "apply to real life", "slow down"] },
  { id: "p1-07", topic: "Music", question: "What kind of music do you listen to?", answer: "Depends on what I'm doing. When I study, I go for soft instrumental music, 'cause lyrics can really distract me. When I'm working out or travelling, I prefer upbeat pop, 'cause it gives me energy and makes the trip more fun.", phrases: ["depends on what I am doing", "can be distracting", "upbeat pop songs"] },
  { id: "p1-08", topic: "Films", question: "Do you prefer watching films at home or in a cinema?", answer: "I generally prefer watching at home, 'cause it's more comfortable and flexible. I can pause, make my own snacks, and skip the trip. But for a big visual film, the cinema screen is still worth the extra effort.", phrases: ["comfortable and flexible", "visually impressive", "worth the extra effort"] },
  { id: "p1-09", topic: "Cooking", question: "How often do you cook?", answer: "I cook several times a week, mostly simple stuff like noodles, vegetables, and chicken. Home cooking is healthier and usually cheaper than ordering in. I'm no expert, but I enjoy tweaking a recipe until it fits my taste.", phrases: ["several times a week", "ordering food", "suits my own taste"] },
  { id: "p1-10", topic: "Exercise", question: "What kind of exercise do you enjoy?", answer: "I enjoy brisk walking and basic strength training. Neither needs expensive gear, so they're easy to fit into my schedule. Exercise also clears my head after hours of studying, which is as valuable to me as the physical side.", phrases: ["brisk walking", "fit into my schedule", "clears my mind"] },
  { id: "p1-11", topic: "Walking", question: "Do you walk a lot in your daily life?", answer: "Yeah, whenever the distance is reasonable, I'd rather walk than take a car. It gives me a chance to notice small changes in the neighbourhood and get some light exercise. I find a twenty-minute walk after a meal especially useful.", phrases: ["whenever the distance is reasonable", "light exercise", "after a meal"] },
  { id: "p1-12", topic: "Public transport", question: "Do you often use public transport?", answer: "Yeah, I use it pretty regularly, 'cause it's affordable and I don't have to worry about parking. The main downside is it can get insanely crowded at rush hour. Still, it's usually the most practical way to get across the city.", phrases: ["quite regularly", "the main drawback", "the most practical way"] },
  { id: "p1-13", topic: "Weather", question: "What kind of weather do you like?", answer: "I like mild, sunny weather with a light breeze. It's comfortable enough to be outside without getting too hot. That kind of weather also puts me in a better mood and makes everyday stuff like commuting feel less tiring.", phrases: ["a light breeze", "outdoor activities", "improves my mood"] },
  { id: "p1-14", topic: "Seasons", question: "What is your favourite season?", answer: "Autumn's my favourite, 'cause the temperature's comfortable and the air often feels fresher. I also love watching the leaves change colour. More importantly, autumn feels like a fresh start after the long summer.", phrases: ["the temperature is comfortable", "changing colours", "a sense of a new beginning"] },
  { id: "p1-15", topic: "Colours", question: "What colour do you like most?", answer: "I'm really into dark blue. It looks calm and professional, and it's easy to match with other colours. I use it for clothes and phone backgrounds, 'cause it's softer than black but still feels simple and reliable.", phrases: ["particularly fond of", "easy to match", "simple and reliable"] },
  { id: "p1-16", topic: "Photos", question: "Do you often take photos?", answer: "I take photos when something feels worth remembering, not everything in sight. Most of my pictures are of meals, trips, or just ordinary moments with friends. Looking back at them helps me remember details I'd otherwise forget.", phrases: ["worth remembering", "ordinary moments", "looking back at them"] },
  { id: "p1-17", topic: "Apps", question: "What app do you use most often?", answer: "I probably use a notes app the most. I keep study plans, useful English expressions, and quick ideas all in one place. It's a simple tool, but being able to search and organise everything saves me a surprising amount of time.", phrases: ["keep ... in one place", "search and organise", "a surprising amount of time"] },
  { id: "p1-18", topic: "Social media", question: "Do you spend much time on social media?", answer: "I use social media every day, but I try to keep it to short bursts. It's good for staying in touch and finding new stuff, but endless scrolling can eat up your time. I've turned off most notifications to cut down the temptation.", phrases: ["limit it to short periods", "endless scrolling", "reduce that temptation"] },
  { id: "p1-19", topic: "Handwriting", question: "Do you prefer writing by hand or typing?", answer: "For long documents, I'd definitely rather type, 'cause it's faster and easier to edit. But I still write study notes by hand. The slower pace forces me to pick out what matters, so I actually remember handwritten notes better.", phrases: ["easier to edit", "forces me to select", "handwritten notes"] },
  { id: "p1-20", topic: "Gifts", question: "Do you enjoy giving gifts to other people?", answer: "Yeah, especially when I know the person well enough to pick something meaningful. I prefer practical or personal gifts over expensive ones. A carefully chosen book or something tied to a shared memory feels way more thoughtful.", phrases: ["something meaningful", "practical or personal", "a shared memory"] },
  { id: "p1-21", topic: "Birthdays", question: "How do you usually celebrate your birthday?", answer: "I usually keep my birthday pretty low-key. I'll have a meal with family or a few close friends and just talk. I don't need a big party; what matters is an unhurried evening with people I care about.", phrases: ["quite low-key", "a few close friends", "an unhurried evening"] },
  { id: "p1-22", topic: "Friends", question: "What do you usually do with your friends?", answer: "We usually meet up for a meal or coffee and catch up on what's been going on. Sometimes we wander around a new neighbourhood or check out an exhibition. The activity isn't the point; I mostly value the conversation and time together.", phrases: ["catch up on", "the activity itself", "shared time"] },
  { id: "p1-23", topic: "Neighbours", question: "Do you know your neighbours well?", answer: "I know a few of them, though we're not super close. We say hi, collect parcels for each other, and sometimes share food. Those little interactions make the building feel friendlier, safer, and less anonymous.", phrases: ["greet one another", "small interactions", "less anonymous"] },
  { id: "p1-24", topic: "Family", question: "Who are you closest to in your family?", answer: "I'm probably closest to my mum, 'cause we talk openly and often. She listens without judging too quickly and usually gives practical advice. Even when we disagree, I know her suggestions come from real concern.", phrases: ["speak openly", "without judging", "genuine concern"] },
  { id: "p1-25", topic: "Pets", question: "Would you like to have a pet?", answer: "I'd love to have a small dog one day, 'cause dogs are affectionate and they get you moving. At the moment, though, my schedule's not stable enough. I'd only get a pet once I could give it consistent care and attention.", phrases: ["one day", "my schedule is not stable enough", "consistent care and attention"] },
  { id: "p1-26", topic: "Plants", question: "Do you keep plants at home?", answer: "Yeah, I keep two small green plants by the window. They barely need any care, but they make the room feel fresher and more welcoming. Looking after them is also a quiet little routine that gives me a break from screens.", phrases: ["require very little care", "more welcoming", "a break from screens"] },
  { id: "p1-27", topic: "Parks", question: "Do you often go to parks?", answer: "I go to a nearby park once or twice a week, usually late afternoon. It's got a long walking path and lots of trees. Even half an hour there helps me reset after a busy or mentally draining day.", phrases: ["once or twice a week", "plenty of trees", "helps me reset"] },
  { id: "p1-28", topic: "Museums", question: "Do you enjoy visiting museums?", answer: "Yeah, especially museums with interactive displays or strong stories behind the objects. They make history feel more real than a textbook. I prefer going slowly and focusing on a few exhibits instead of trying to see everything.", phrases: ["interactive displays", "feel more concrete", "focusing on a few exhibits"] },
  { id: "p1-29", topic: "Libraries", question: "Do you use libraries?", answer: "I mainly use libraries as quiet places to study. The serious atmosphere helps me concentrate, and being surrounded by books makes me less likely to check my phone. I also like that libraries give you useful space without expecting you to spend money.", phrases: ["quiet places to study", "helps me concentrate", "without expecting visitors to spend money"] },
  { id: "p1-30", topic: "Cafés", question: "Do you like studying in cafés?", answer: "I sometimes study in cafés when I need a change of scenery. A moderate level of background noise can actually help me focus. But I only pick cafés with enough space and avoid rush hours, 'cause constant movement gets distracting.", phrases: ["a change of environment", "background noise", "becomes distracting"] },
  { id: "p1-31", topic: "Shopping", question: "Do you enjoy shopping?", answer: "I enjoy shopping when I know what I need, but I hate wandering around without a purpose. I usually compare options online before hitting the shops. That makes the whole thing quicker and less likely to end in a pointless purchase.", phrases: ["without a purpose", "compare options", "something unnecessary"] },
  { id: "p1-32", topic: "Clothes", question: "What kind of clothes do you usually wear?", answer: "I usually wear simple, comfortable clothes in neutral colours. I prefer stuff that can be mixed and matched, instead of chasing every trend. For formal occasions, I go for a well-fitted jacket, 'cause it instantly makes an outfit look sharper.", phrases: ["neutral colours", "following every trend", "look more polished"] },
  { id: "p1-33", topic: "Shoes", question: "Is it important for you to wear comfortable shoes?", answer: "Yeah, comfort comes first, 'cause I walk a lot. Uncomfortable shoes can ruin an otherwise great day. I still care how they look, but I usually go for a simple design that matches different outfits and actually supports my feet.", phrases: ["my first priority", "ruin an otherwise enjoyable day", "supports my feet"] },
  { id: "p1-34", topic: "Bags", question: "What do you usually carry in your bag?", answer: "I normally carry my phone, wallet, earphones, a reusable bottle, and a small notebook. If I'm heading out to study, I'll also bring a tablet or laptop. I try not to carry too much, 'cause a heavy bag gets annoying fast.", phrases: ["a reusable bottle", "if I am going to study", "quickly becomes inconvenient"] },
  { id: "p1-35", topic: "Watches", question: "Do you wear a watch?", answer: "Yeah, I wear a simple digital watch most days. It lets me check the time without grabbing my phone and getting pulled into messages. I also use the timer when I study, so it's practical, not just for looks.", phrases: ["without picking up my phone", "getting distracted", "a fashion accessory"] },
  { id: "p1-36", topic: "Chocolate", question: "Do you like chocolate?", answer: "Yeah, especially dark chocolate, 'cause it's less sweet and has a richer flavour. I usually have a small piece with coffee instead of a whole bar. It feels like a little reward after finishing something hard.", phrases: ["a richer flavour", "rather than a whole bar", "a simple reward"] },
  { id: "p1-37", topic: "Tea and coffee", question: "Do you prefer tea or coffee?", answer: "I prefer coffee in the morning, 'cause the smell and taste help me wake up. In the evening I usually go for tea, since it's gentler and more relaxing. So honestly, it just depends on the time of day.", phrases: ["help me feel alert", "gentler and more relaxing", "depends on the time of day"] },
  { id: "p1-38", topic: "Sleep", question: "How many hours do you usually sleep?", answer: "I try to get around seven or eight hours, though that doesn't always happen during busy periods. When I sleep well, my focus and mood are noticeably better. So I try to put my phone away at least half an hour before bed.", phrases: ["during busy periods", "noticeably better", "half an hour before bed"] },
  { id: "p1-39", topic: "Concentration", question: "What helps you concentrate?", answer: "A clear desk and a short task list help me concentrate the most. I put my phone out of reach and work in focused blocks with small breaks. Knowing exactly what I need to finish stops me from feeling overwhelmed by a big goal.", phrases: ["a clear desk", "focused blocks", "feeling overwhelmed"] },
  { id: "p1-40", topic: "Patience", question: "Are you a patient person?", answer: "I'm patient with long-term goals, but less so with pointless delays. For example, I can practise a skill for months if I see gradual progress. But waiting in a badly organised queue really gets to me, 'cause the delay feels so avoidable.", phrases: ["long-term goals", "gradual progress", "feels avoidable"] },
  { id: "p1-41", topic: "Laughter", question: "What kinds of things make you laugh?", answer: "I laugh most at quick, unexpected observations rather than prepared jokes. Conversations with close friends are often funny, 'cause we know the background behind every comment. Light comedy videos also help me unwind after a stressful day.", phrases: ["unexpected observations", "understand the background", "a stressful day"] },
  { id: "p1-42", topic: "Helping others", question: "Do you often help other people?", answer: "I try to help when the request is specific and I know I can actually be useful. That might mean explaining a digital tool, reviewing a document, or just listening. Small, practical help often beats general advice.", phrases: ["the request is specific", "simply listening", "small, practical help"] },
  { id: "p1-43", topic: "Noise", question: "What kinds of noise do you dislike?", answer: "I can't stand repetitive mechanical noise, like drilling or a car alarm, 'cause it's impossible to ignore. Normal conversation doesn't bother me as much. When I need to focus, I just use simple earplugs instead of blasting music.", phrases: ["repetitive mechanical noise", "difficult to ignore", "does not bother me"] },
  { id: "p1-44", topic: "Crowded places", question: "Do you like crowded places?", answer: "Only in certain situations. A busy festival can feel exciting, 'cause everyone's sharing the same good energy, but crowded transport is tiring and uncomfortable. Day to day, I usually pick quieter times to shop or visit popular places.", phrases: ["in certain situations", "positive energy", "choose quieter times"] },
  { id: "p1-45", topic: "Maps", question: "Do you often use maps?", answer: "Yeah, digital maps are a must whenever I'm in an unfamiliar area. I use them not just for directions, but also to compare travel times and find nearby cafés. Still, I try to remember major landmarks instead of staring at the screen the whole time.", phrases: ["an unfamiliar area", "compare travel times", "major landmarks"] },
  { id: "p1-46", topic: "Languages", question: "Why are you learning English?", answer: "English gives me access to more education, work opportunities, and international information. Right now my main reason is hitting my IELTS target, but I also want to communicate more confidently when I travel or work with people from different backgrounds.", phrases: ["gives me access to", "my immediate reason", "different backgrounds"] },
  { id: "p1-47", topic: "Numbers", question: "Are you good at remembering numbers?", answer: "I'm fairly good with short numbers if there's a pattern, but random strings are hard. I usually break a long number into smaller groups, or link it to a familiar date. That makes it way easier to recall.", phrases: ["have a pattern", "smaller groups", "easier to recall"] },
  { id: "p1-48", topic: "Science", question: "Did you enjoy science at school?", answer: "Yeah, especially biology, 'cause it explained things I could actually see in everyday life. Physics was harder, but hands-on experiments made it interesting. A good demo often helped me get an idea faster than reading a long explanation.", phrases: ["observe in everyday life", "practical experiments", "a good demonstration"] },
  { id: "p1-49", topic: "History", question: "Do you think learning history is important?", answer: "Yeah, 'cause history shows how today's institutions and conflicts developed. It also reminds us that social change usually has complicated causes. I don't remember every date, but understanding the bigger story helps me read current events more carefully.", phrases: ["present-day institutions", "complex causes", "the wider story"] },
  { id: "p1-50", topic: "Future plans", question: "What would you like to do in the next few years?", answer: "My main goal is to hit the IELTS score I need and keep building useful professional skills. I'd also like to travel more independently. Instead of a rigid five-year plan, I prefer setting a clear direction and checking in on it regularly.", phrases: ["my main goal", "travel more independently", "a clear direction"] },
];

const CORE_PART2_CUES: Part2Cue[] = [
  { id: "p2-01", topic: "A useful skill", prompt: "Describe a skill you learned that was useful to you.", bullets: ["what the skill was", "when and how you learned it", "why you decided to learn it", "how it has helped you"], answer: "So, the skill I'd like to talk about is touch typing, which I learned back in my first year of university. At the time, I had to write these really long essays, but I typed super slowly and kept staring at the keyboard the whole time. Every essay probably took me twice as long as it should've, and honestly, I'd lose my train of thought just hunting for letters. So I started using this free training website for about twenty minutes every evening, and I focused on accuracy first, not speed. The first few weeks were rough, 'cause my hands felt so unnatural, and my speed actually got worse before it got better. But after about two months, I could type without even looking down, and it just felt automatic. Now it saves me so much time whenever I'm writing reports or taking notes. And the best part is, I can actually focus on my ideas instead of searching for the keys, so the whole process feels a lot smoother. I'd honestly recommend it to anyone who spends hours in front of a computer, 'cause the effort at the beginning is nothing compared to the time you get back. The first time I typed a whole paragraph without glancing down, it felt like a tiny victory. Looking back, those two months are some of the most useful hours I've ever invested.", phrases: ["focus on accuracy before speed", "saves me a great deal of time", "concentrate on my ideas"] },
  { id: "p2-02", topic: "An inspiring teacher", prompt: "Describe a teacher who inspired you.", bullets: ["who the teacher was", "what they taught", "what made them special", "how they influenced you"], answer: "The teacher who inspired me the most was my high school English teacher, Ms Li. She taught us grammar and reading, but her classes were never just about the textbook. She'd bring in short news stories and ask us to explain what we thought about them, and I remember one class where we spent the whole hour talking about a single newspaper photo. What made her special was her patience. When someone made a mistake, she treated it as useful information about what to practice next, not as something to be embarrassed about. She once told me that being clear mattered more than having a perfect accent, and honestly, that sentence stayed with me long after I left school. That one comment completely changed my attitude. I stopped being afraid of speaking, started using English outside class, talked to exchange students, kept a little diary in English. Her way of teaching still affects how I study today, 'cause I try to notice my mistakes, fix them, and move on instead of feeling bad about them. I think a good teacher doesn't just change your grades; she changes how you see your own ability.", phrases: ["never limited to the textbook", "changed my attitude completely", "move forward"] },
  { id: "p2-03", topic: "An older person", prompt: "Describe an older person you enjoy talking to.", bullets: ["who the person is", "how you know them", "what you talk about", "why you enjoy the conversations"], answer: "I really enjoy talking to my grandfather, who's in his seventies and used to work as an engineer. We usually talk during family meals or when I go over to his place on weekends. He tells me stories about how the city has changed, how people worked before computers were everywhere, and the mistakes he made when he was young. Last month he described how his first factory job meant calculating everything by hand, and I realized how much patience that kind of work took. I love these conversations because he never forces advice on you. Instead, he just tells you about a real experience and lets you draw your own conclusion. He's also genuinely curious about new technology, so our talks go both ways: he explains the past to me, and I show him how modern apps work. We actually spent an afternoon together comparing an old mechanical calculator with a phone app. I always leave those talks with a wider perspective, and honestly, he seems energised too. These chats remind me that understanding a different generation isn't about agreeing with everything, it's about actually listening.", phrases: ["in a forceful way", "draw my own conclusion", "a wider perspective"] },
  { id: "p2-04", topic: "A close friend", prompt: "Describe a friend who is important to you.", bullets: ["who the friend is", "how you met", "what you do together", "why the friendship matters"], answer: "The friend I'd like to describe is Kevin, who I met in a university group project. At first we were just classmates with totally different working styles. I liked to plan everything early, while he was way more spontaneous, and during our first meeting we actually argued about how to organise the work. But over time, we learned to appreciate those differences and became close friends. He taught me to leave room for new ideas, and I helped him meet deadlines, and our project ended up being one of the best in the class. We often grab coffee, talk about our plans, and take long walks around the city. The friendship matters because he's both honest and supportive. If I have some unrealistic idea, he tells me straight, but then he helps me find a better option. We don't need to talk every day to stay close. Whenever we meet, the conversation just picks up naturally, and I feel completely comfortable being myself around him. I honestly believe friendships formed through shared work can be just as strong as the ones from childhood.", phrases: ["very different working styles", "honest and supportive", "being completely myself"] },
  { id: "p2-05", topic: "A family member", prompt: "Describe a family member you admire.", bullets: ["who the person is", "what they are like", "what they have achieved", "why you admire them"], answer: "The family member I admire the most is my mom. She's calm, practical, and incredibly dependable. For years, she balanced a demanding job with looking after the family, but she rarely made anyone feel like she was too busy to listen. I admire her less for one big achievement and more for the way she handles ordinary responsibilities, day after day. When problems show up, she breaks them into smaller steps and focuses on what can actually be changed. I remember once when we hit a sudden financial difficulty, she just wrote down every expense, cut what wasn't necessary, and quietly rebuilt our savings over two years. I've copied that habit in my own studies, when a task feels overwhelming, I split it up and deal with one part at a time. She's also kept learning new digital skills instead of using her age as an excuse. Her example taught me that strength is often quiet, it means staying patient, keeping your promises, and helping others without needing credit. Whenever I get impatient with slow progress, I remember her steady style and try to match it, 'cause she rarely talks about her own efforts, which makes her example even more powerful.", phrases: ["remarkably dependable", "ordinary responsibilities", "without needing recognition"] },
  { id: "p2-06", topic: "A famous person", prompt: "Describe a famous person you would like to meet.", bullets: ["who the person is", "what they are known for", "why you want to meet them", "what you would ask"], answer: "If I could meet anyone, it would be the filmmaker Hayao Miyazaki. He's known for animated films that mix imaginative worlds with thoughtful themes about childhood, nature, and responsibility. I'd love to meet him because his work proves that stories can be entertaining without being shallow. His films have these small, quiet moments that feel just as important as the dramatic scenes, like a character simply watching the wind move through a field. If I ever got the chance, I'd ask how he protects an original idea when you have a big team and commercial pressure, because that's a problem in a lot of creative industries. I'd also ask why nature shows up so often in his stories, and whether he sees it as a source of comfort or of warning. I imagine he's probably a private person, so I wouldn't expect a long conversation. Even a short explanation of his creative process would be memorable. Honestly, his films made me realize that animation can tackle serious questions without losing its sense of wonder. And honestly, that's the kind of influence I'd love to have one day, the ability to make people think while they're being entertained.", phrases: ["entertaining without being shallow", "commercial pressure", "creative process"] },
  { id: "p2-07", topic: "A different culture", prompt: "Describe a person from a different cultural background.", bullets: ["who the person is", "where they are from", "how you met", "what you learned from them"], answer: "I once took a short online course with a classmate from Spain named Marta, and we ended up working on a presentation together. At first, I noticed she disagreed way more directly than I was used to, but she did it warmly and never made it personal. In one meeting she told me plainly that my part of the presentation was too vague, and then she spent twenty minutes helping me make it specific. She also treated meal times as proper social occasions rather than quick breaks, and once she actually paused our discussion so everyone could eat. Through those conversations, I learned that communication habits can look rude or overly indirect when you take them out of their cultural context. Marta encouraged me to ask for clarification instead of judging right away. We don't work together anymore, but that experience made me much more patient in international teams and more curious about why people communicate differently. Now I see cultural differences as something to understand, not something to judge. Looking back, that course taught me more about people than about the subject itself, and I'm honestly grateful we were placed in the same group.", phrases: ["expressed disagreement directly", "cultural context", "ask for clarification"] },
  { id: "p2-08", topic: "A helpful community member", prompt: "Describe someone who helps other people in your community.", bullets: ["who the person is", "what they do", "who they help", "why their work matters"], answer: "There's this retired woman in my neighbourhood called Mrs Wang, and she runs a small volunteer group. Every week, the group checks on older residents who live alone, helps them get their medicine, and teaches them how to use basic phone services. Her work matters because so many everyday systems have moved online, and that can leave some elderly people feeling totally helpless. I once watched her spend half an hour with an eighty-year-old man who was terrified of making a payment on his phone, and instead of taking over, she guided his hand through each step until he did it alone. Mrs Wang doesn't just complete tasks for people, she explains every step patiently so they can become more independent. She also connects neighbours who might never have spoken otherwise, organising small gatherings that turn strangers into familiar faces. I admire the fact that the project is simple and local, but the effect is very real. It turns a group of apartment buildings into something closer to an actual community. And you know what, I've joined the group a couple of times myself now, and you can really see how much those older residents look forward to the visits.", phrases: ["checks on older residents", "become more independent", "simple and local"] },
  { id: "p2-09", topic: "Someone good at their job", prompt: "Describe a person who is very good at their job.", bullets: ["who the person is", "what job they do", "what skills they have", "why you think they are good"], answer: "A person who's excellent at her job is the dentist I've been seeing for years. Dental treatment makes a lot of people nervous, but she explains what she's going to do before she starts and keeps checking whether you're comfortable. I still remember my first visit, when she noticed I was gripping the chair and paused the whole procedure just to let me breathe. Her technical skill is obvious because she works carefully and efficiently, but honestly, it's her communication that really stands out. She never uses complicated medical language when a simple explanation will do, and she always tells you what a strange sound or sensation means before it happens. She also remembers small details from earlier visits, which builds trust. I think being good at a job involves more than producing the right result. It also means understanding how the patient experiences the whole process, and she handles both sides incredibly well. Her example actually changed how I think about professionalism in any field. And that's actually changed how I treat service workers in my own life, because I try to explain what I need clearly and remember that the person on the other side is doing their best.", phrases: ["what really stands out", "creates trust", "manages both sides"] },
  { id: "p2-10", topic: "A child", prompt: "Describe a child you enjoyed spending time with.", bullets: ["who the child was", "when you spent time together", "what you did", "why you enjoyed it"], answer: "I spent a really nice afternoon with my cousin's six-year-old daughter during a family visit. We went to a park and collected leaves in all different shapes and colours, and she treated every single leaf like it was a rare discovery. She asked a constant stream of questions, including some I couldn't answer, like why certain trees lose their leaves earlier than others. Later we used the leaves to make a simple picture at home, arranging them into a bird and a sun with glue and crayons. I enjoyed the time because she paid full attention to things adults normally ignore. A small insect or an unusual stone could hold her interest for several minutes, and she noticed details in the park that I'd walked past for years. Her curiosity reminded me that an ordinary place can become interesting when you slow down and actually observe it. I was tired by the end, sure, but in a really positive way, and I left with a fresh appreciation for simple activities. That afternoon also reminded me that kids often teach adults how to be present, and honestly, I've started leaving my phone at home during short walks, just to observe the way she does.", phrases: ["a constant stream of questions", "hold her interest", "slow down and observe"] },
  { id: "p2-11", topic: "A quiet place", prompt: "Describe a quiet place you like to visit.", bullets: ["where it is", "how often you go", "what you do there", "why you like it"], answer: "A quiet place I really like is a small public library about fifteen minutes from my home. It's on the second floor of an older building, with big windows facing a row of trees, so the reading area gets beautiful natural light. I go there once or twice a week when I need to study without interruptions. I usually grab a desk in the corner, put my phone away, and work for two focused hours. Before I walk in, I set one clear goal for the session, because otherwise the time just slips away without producing anything useful. I like this library because the atmosphere pushes you to concentrate without feeling strict. Everyone around you is reading or working quietly, so there's this gentle sense of shared purpose that I rarely feel in a café. And unlike a café, I don't have to buy anything or listen to background music, and nobody expects me to leave after a certain time. I normally walk out feeling calmer and more organised. Honestly, it's become my default answer whenever someone asks where I go to think, and I hope such spaces stay available for everyone who needs them.", phrases: ["without interruption", "a sense of shared purpose", "calmer and more organised"] },
  { id: "p2-12", topic: "A crowded place", prompt: "Describe a crowded place you have been to.", bullets: ["where it was", "when you went", "why it was crowded", "how you felt"], answer: "The most crowded place I remember was a railway station just before the Spring Festival. I was travelling home, and it felt like thousands of other people had the exact same plan for the same evening. The waiting area was packed, you could barely hear the announcements, and everyone was carrying big bags, suitcases, or boxes of gifts for their families. At first I was really stressed, because I was worried about missing my train, and I kept checking my ticket every few minutes. But the staff created clear queues, and several passengers helped each other lift heavy luggage onto the platform. A stranger even shared his little stool with an elderly woman while we waited. Once I got to my seat, I could just relax and let my shoulders drop. The experience was exhausting, sure, but it also showed me the scale of holiday travel in China and how many people quietly cooperate under pressure. I'd probably avoid that time of year if I could, though the shared excitement of people going home was honestly quite moving. And honestly, I came away with a new respect for the people who run those stations during the busiest weeks of the year, and for the patience of the passengers themselves.", phrases: ["just before the Spring Festival", "clear queues", "shared excitement"] },
  { id: "p2-13", topic: "A natural place", prompt: "Describe a natural place you would like to visit.", bullets: ["where it is", "how you know about it", "what you would do there", "why you want to visit"], answer: "A place I'd love to visit is Zhangjiajie National Forest Park in Hunan. I first saw its tall sandstone pillars in a documentary, and I couldn't believe the landscape looked almost imaginary, like a painting rather than a real place. If I went there, I'd spend several days walking the quieter trails and take the cable car to see the formations from above, because apparently the view from the top is completely different from ground level. I'd also go early in the morning, when mist often sits between the peaks and the scenery changes every few minutes. The main reason I want to visit is to experience a landscape completely different from the flat city environment I see every day. Photos are impressive, but they can't really show you the scale, the temperature, the sounds, or what it feels like to stand under something enormous. I think being there would make the geography feel real in a way no screen can reproduce, and it would probably change how I understand my own country. Plus, it'd be a great chance to practice photography, since the changing light would force me to wait for the right moment.", phrases: ["sandstone pillars", "quieter trails", "communicate the scale"] },
  { id: "p2-14", topic: "A historical building", prompt: "Describe a historical building you have visited.", bullets: ["what the building is", "where it is", "what you saw there", "why it impressed you"], answer: "A historical building that really impressed me was the Palace Museum in Beijing. I visited on a cool autumn morning and walked in through those enormous red gates, and honestly, even the entrance alone made me stop and look up. Inside, the courtyards just kept going, one after another, each with carefully painted roofs and stone details that had clearly been maintained for centuries. What impressed me most wasn't any single room, it was the scale and symmetry of the whole complex. You could see how architecture was used to express authority and order, because every path seemed to lead toward the centre. I also noticed small signs of daily life, like heating structures and narrow side passages, and that made the palace feel less like a textbook image and more like a place where people actually lived. The visit gave me a physical sense of history, because I was walking through spaces that had been used for hundreds of years. I'd happily go again, especially in a quieter season. And I learned that a place can communicate meaning through its layout alone, without anyone explaining a single object to you.", phrases: ["the scale and symmetry", "express authority", "a physical sense of history"] },
  { id: "p2-15", topic: "A city", prompt: "Describe a city you would like to live in for a short time.", bullets: ["which city it is", "what you know about it", "what you would do there", "why only for a short time"], answer: "I'd love to live in Melbourne for about six months. The city is known for its multicultural food, public transport, and strong arts scene, and friends who've visited always describe it as relaxed but active. I'd rent a small place near a tram line, explore different neighbourhoods on weekends, and join a local English conversation group. Living there would give me a chance to use English in ordinary situations instead of just in lessons, which is exactly the kind of practice I need. I say a short time because moving permanently is a much bigger decision involving work, family, and long-term costs. Six months would be long enough to build a routine and understand the city beyond its tourist attractions, but short enough to stay flexible and test whether I'd actually enjoy that life. I think it would be both a language experience and a useful test of independence. And honestly, the idea of arriving somewhere new and building a small daily life from nothing still excites me more than any holiday. So yeah, if I get the chance, Melbourne would probably be my first choice, because it feels like a city where you could actually build a life, not just visit one.", phrases: ["strong arts scene", "ordinary situations", "a useful test of independence"] },
  { id: "p2-16", topic: "A foreign country", prompt: "Describe a country you would like to visit.", bullets: ["which country it is", "what you know about it", "what you would like to do", "why it interests you"], answer: "Japan is a country I'd really like to visit, especially in autumn. I'm interested in the contrast between super efficient modern cities and carefully preserved traditional places, and Japan seems to manage both at the same time. I'd spend a few days in Tokyo, then take the train to Kyoto and a smaller town, because the journey between them is supposed to be beautiful in itself. I'd love to visit local markets, try regional food, and observe how public spaces are organised, since I find the design of everyday objects genuinely fascinating. What interests me is that so many everyday things there seem designed with great attention to detail, from station signs to packaging. I also enjoy Japanese films and animation, so seeing the landscapes behind those stories would be really meaningful. I'd plan the trip carefully but leave plenty of free time to wander and find places that aren't on a standard itinerary, because those unexpected discoveries are usually the most memorable part of any journey. And the food, I suspect what I've tasted at home is only a simplified version of the real thing.", phrases: ["the contrast between", "attention to detail", "a standard itinerary"] },
  { id: "p2-17", topic: "A favourite room", prompt: "Describe a room in your home that you like.", bullets: ["which room it is", "what it looks like", "what you do there", "why you like it"], answer: "My favourite room is the small study next to my bedroom. It's got a simple wooden desk, a comfortable chair, and two narrow shelves, which is probably enough furniture for what I actually do there. The walls are light-coloured, and the window gets afternoon sunlight, so the room feels open even though it's quite small. I use it for studying, reading, and planning my week. What I like most is that every object has a clear purpose, so the space rarely feels crowded. I keep my phone outside when I need to concentrate, and I've got one green plant near the window to make the room feel less formal. There's also a small noticeboard where I pin my weekly goals, and seeing them in front of me helps me stay on track. It's not the biggest or most impressive room in the apartment, but it gives me a strong mental signal that it's time to focus. The moment I sit down, my mind just seems to switch into work mode. Honestly, the room has taught me that a small, well-chosen space can support focus better than a large room full of distractions.", phrases: ["every object has a clear purpose", "a mental signal", "it is time to focus"] },
  { id: "p2-18", topic: "A place to study", prompt: "Describe a place where you study effectively.", bullets: ["where it is", "what the place is like", "how you use it", "why it works well"], answer: "I study most effectively at a desk beside the window in my local library. The desk is wide enough for my laptop, notebook, and water bottle, but there's no extra space for clutter, which stops me from spreading unnecessary stuff around. I usually book a two-hour slot, decide on one clear goal, and work in fifty-minute blocks with short breaks in between. The place works well because it's quiet without being completely silent, the soft sounds of pages turning and people moving are actually calming. Seeing other people concentrate also makes me less likely to check my phone. The natural light keeps me from feeling sleepy, and I can take a short walk between sessions to clear my head. Over time, I've built an association between that particular desk and focused work, so I can settle down quickly even when I didn't feel motivated beforehand. I've tried studying at home, but the library is simply more reliable, and honestly, I now treat my reservation there almost like an appointment. My focus improves just because the desk is used for one purpose. I've noticed that my focus improves simply because the desk is used for one purpose, and I rarely check the time unless my timer reminds me.", phrases: ["no extra space for clutter", "work in blocks", "built an association"] },
  { id: "p2-19", topic: "A park", prompt: "Describe a park you often visit.", bullets: ["where the park is", "what it looks like", "what people do there", "why you visit it"], answer: "The park I visit most often is a riverside park near my apartment. It's got a wide path for walking and cycling, a few open lawns, and a line of trees that provides shade in summer, so it works for exercise, rest, and everything in between. In the mornings, older people exercise there, while families and runners usually show up later in the day, and somehow everyone shares the space without conflict. I mainly go there to walk after long study sessions. The route is long enough to clear my mind but familiar enough that I don't need to plan anything. I especially love the section beside the water, where the traffic noise fades and you can watch the light change on the river. I often bring a book and read on a bench for half an hour before walking home. The park isn't a famous attraction, yet it improves my daily life because it offers an easy way to spend time outdoors without travelling far. It's also become a kind of mental bookmark for me, because returning to the same bench often helps me pick up an old thought or decision exactly where I left it.", phrases: ["provides shade", "clear my mind", "improves my daily life"] },
  { id: "p2-20", topic: "A shop", prompt: "Describe a shop you like visiting.", bullets: ["what kind of shop it is", "where it is", "what it sells", "why you like it"], answer: "I love visiting this small independent bookshop near a university campus. It sells new books, second-hand titles, and a carefully selected range of notebooks, and the stock changes often enough that a visit never feels wasted. The shop is narrow, with tall shelves and handwritten recommendation cards from the staff, which gives it a personality that chain stores just don't have. I enjoy it because the selection feels personal rather than controlled by sales rankings. The owner often asks what I've enjoyed recently and suggests one or two books instead of pushing the most expensive option. There's also a quiet corner where customers can read for a few minutes before deciding, and nobody pressures you to hurry. I've discovered several writers there that I'd never have found by searching online. Online shopping is faster, sure, but this shop turns buying a book into a discovery process, and I often walk out with something I'd never heard of before. Honestly, that surprise is exactly what makes it worth visiting, and I've started buying birthday presents there too, because a signed second-hand edition feels far more personal than a standard online gift.", phrases: ["carefully selected", "sales rankings", "a discovery process"] },
  { id: "p2-21", topic: "A useful object", prompt: "Describe an object you use every day.", bullets: ["what it is", "when you got it", "how you use it", "why it is useful"], answer: "An object I use every single day is a pair of noise-cancelling headphones that I bought about two years ago. I originally got them for travel, but they quickly became part of my study routine, and honestly, now I rarely leave home without them. I wear them in cafes, on public transport, and at home when there's construction noise nearby, because they turn a noisy environment into something I can control. Sometimes I play soft instrumental music, but a lot of the time I just use the noise-cancelling function and enjoy the silence. They're useful because they help me create a consistent working environment in places I can't control, which matters a lot when your home isn't always quiet. The headphones were fairly expensive, so I hesitated for a while and spent a week comparing models. But I've used them for hundreds of hours now, and the improvement in concentration has made the purchase totally worthwhile. If they broke tomorrow, I'd definitely buy another pair. They've also become a small ritual of preparation, because putting them on signals to my brain that serious work is about to begin.", phrases: ["part of my study routine", "a consistent working environment", "made the purchase worthwhile"] },
  { id: "p2-22", topic: "A meaningful gift", prompt: "Describe a gift that was important to you.", bullets: ["what the gift was", "who gave it to you", "when you received it", "why it was meaningful"], answer: "A gift that really meant something to me was a fountain pen from my dad, given when I graduated from university. It's dark blue with a very simple design, and he had my initials engraved on the cap, so it clearly belonged to me from the very first moment. The pen wasn't something I'd asked for, which made it more memorable, because it showed he'd thought about what a graduate might actually need. He told me he hoped I'd use it to sign important documents and record good ideas, and I remember him saying that a good tool can last longer than any job. Now I mainly use it for weekly planning and personal notes rather than everyday quick writing, which keeps it special. The pen is meaningful because it marks the transition from student life to greater independence, and it shows up in almost every important page of my notebooks. It also reminds me of my dad's quiet way of showing support, because he rarely says sentimental things directly. I expect to keep it for many years, even though I use digital tools more often, and I've started a small tradition of writing one meaningful line with it at the start of every year.", phrases: ["engraved on the cap", "marks the transition", "expressing support"] },
  { id: "p2-23", topic: "A favourite photo", prompt: "Describe a photo you like.", bullets: ["what is in the photo", "when it was taken", "who took it", "why you like it"], answer: "One photo I really like shows my family sitting around a table during a holiday meal. It was taken by my cousin on an ordinary phone, so the lighting isn't perfect, and two people are looking away from the camera at the exact moment the picture was taken. And honestly, that's exactly why I like it. The picture feels natural rather than carefully arranged, because nobody had time to prepare their best angle. My grandfather is laughing at something outside the frame, and everyone else has a completely different expression, which makes the photo feel alive. It was taken before several family members moved to other cities, so having everyone together now feels especially valuable, and the photo has become a kind of record of that full table. Whenever I see it, I remember the noise, the food, and the conversation, not just the visual image. It captures an ordinary moment that became more meaningful over time. I've promised myself to take more pictures like this instead of only photographing special occasions, because the slightly imperfect ones often carry the most genuine emotion.", phrases: ["outside the frame", "carefully arranged", "became more meaningful with time"] },
  { id: "p2-24", topic: "A book", prompt: "Describe a book that influenced you.", bullets: ["what the book is", "when you read it", "what it is about", "how it influenced you"], answer: "A book that really influenced me is Atomic Habits by James Clear. I read it when I was struggling to keep a regular study routine, and my desk was covered with unfinished plans that never lasted more than a week. The book explains how small repeated actions shape long-term results, and how your environment can make a habit easier or harder, which sounds simple but is surprisingly easy to ignore. The idea that helped me most was to focus on systems rather than relying on motivation, because motivation naturally disappears on difficult days. After reading it, I started leaving my study materials ready on the desk and setting a minimum task of just ten minutes. That made starting much easier, and ten minutes often turned into a full session once I was already sitting there. The book didn't give me some dramatic overnight change, but it helped me design routines that still work when I'm tired or busy. I've recommended it to several friends, and I reread a few chapters whenever my habits start to slip. What surprised me most was that the method felt almost too simple, yet it worked, which made me trust practical systems more than grand promises.", phrases: ["shape long-term results", "focus on systems", "a dramatic overnight change"] },
  { id: "p2-25", topic: "A film", prompt: "Describe a film that made you think.", bullets: ["what the film is", "when you watched it", "what it is about", "why it made you think"], answer: "A film that really made me think was The Truman Show. It's about a guy whose entire life is secretly produced as a TV programme, while everyone around him is an actor, and he slowly discovers that nothing around him is real. I first watched it just for entertainment, but the central idea stayed with me long after the credits rolled. The film raises questions about privacy, freedom, and how much of our behaviour is shaped by other people's expectations, and honestly, those questions feel even more relevant now because people voluntarily share so much of their lives online. What really hit me was Truman's decision to leave a safe but artificial world, even though he had no idea what was outside it, because choosing uncertainty over comfort is never easy. The film is funny and easy to watch, yet it pushes you to examine whether comfort is always the same as happiness. It made me look at my own daily choices and ask how many of them are genuinely mine rather than simply expected of me. I've watched it twice more since then, and each time I notice a different detail, which just proves that a good film grows with you.", phrases: ["raises questions about", "even more relevant", "a safe but artificial world"] },
  { id: "p2-26", topic: "A song", prompt: "Describe a song that is special to you.", bullets: ["what the song is", "when you first heard it", "what it sounds like", "why it is special"], answer: "A song that's special to me is Here Comes the Sun by the Beatles. I first heard it in a film when I was a teenager, and its gentle guitar melody immediately stood out from everything else I was listening to at the time. The song is simple and optimistic without being overly dramatic, which is exactly the mood I need on difficult days. I often play it on hard mornings or after finishing a stressful period of work, and somehow it always makes the situation feel lighter. It reminds me that bad situations usually change gradually, even when progress is hard to notice, kind of like sunlight returning after clouds without announcing itself. I also associate it with a road trip where my friends and I played an old playlist on repeat, and the song became the unofficial soundtrack of that week. Hearing the opening notes brings back the sunlight, the conversation, and the feeling of having no urgent responsibilities for a few hours. Music has this strange power to store entire periods of life inside a few seconds of melody, and this one holds a really happy chapter for me.", phrases: ["immediately stood out", "without sounding overly dramatic", "brings back"] },
  { id: "p2-27", topic: "A useful app", prompt: "Describe an app that you find useful.", bullets: ["what the app is", "how you found it", "how you use it", "why it is useful"], answer: "An app I find genuinely useful is a flashcard app that uses spaced repetition. I found it while looking for a better way to remember English vocabulary, because my old method of writing long lists just didn't work. I create cards with a word, a short definition, and one sentence from a real context, so I always learn words inside a situation rather than in isolation. Then the app decides when each card should appear again, based on how easily I remembered it, which removes all the guesswork from reviewing. This is useful because difficult words come back a lot while familiar ones take less of my time, so I never waste hours on words I already know. I normally review cards for ten minutes during breakfast or on public transport, and honestly, that short daily habit has worked far better than occasional long sessions. The app isn't visually exciting, but it turns a huge learning goal into a small daily task. After a year of using it, I can look back at my statistics and see real progress, which is more motivating than any game or reward the app could offer.", phrases: ["uses spaced repetition", "based on how easily", "a small daily task"] },
  { id: "p2-28", topic: "A website", prompt: "Describe a website you often use.", bullets: ["what the website is", "how you discovered it", "what you use it for", "why you return to it"], answer: "A website I use all the time is the BBC Learning English site. I found it a few years ago while searching for short listening materials, and it's been one of my first stops ever since. It provides news-based lessons, pronunciation videos, and explanations of common expressions, all organised by level, which makes it easy to find something appropriate. I keep coming back because the content is divided into manageable sections and the speakers use clear, natural English rather than slow artificial pronunciation. A typical lesson takes only a few minutes, so I can use it even on a busy day, and I've made it part of my morning routine. I also like that the examples are connected to current topics rather than invented textbook situations, so I learn vocabulary that's actually being used right now. I don't rely on it as my only learning source, but it's a dependable place to practice listening and collect expressions I can later use in speaking. Lately I've also started downloading the transcripts and reading them aloud, which turns the same material into speaking practice instead of just listening practice.", phrases: ["manageable sections", "current topics", "a dependable place"] },
  { id: "p2-29", topic: "Clothing", prompt: "Describe an item of clothing you like wearing.", bullets: ["what it is", "when you got it", "when you wear it", "why you like it"], answer: "An item of clothing I really like is a dark navy jacket that I bought before starting a new job. It has a simple cut and is formal enough for meetings without looking too serious, which is a balance I rarely find in clothes. I wear it with a shirt for work or with a plain T-shirt when I go out, so the same jacket covers both sides of my week. I like it because it fits well and saves me from thinking too much about what to wear, and on busy mornings that's genuinely valuable. The colour also works with almost everything I own, which wasn't planned but turned out to be super convenient. Although it was more expensive than my usual clothes, the fabric has stayed in great condition after years of use, and it still looks neat after being washed many times. For me, a favourite item isn't necessarily fashionable, it's something reliable that makes me feel comfortable and prepared. This jacket has definitely earned its place in my wardrobe, and it's even survived two winters without needing any repairs, which is more than I can say for cheaper jackets I owned before.", phrases: ["a simple cut", "works with almost everything", "comfortable and prepared"] },
  { id: "p2-30", topic: "An old object", prompt: "Describe an old object your family keeps.", bullets: ["what it is", "how old it is", "where it came from", "why your family keeps it"], answer: "My family keeps a mechanical clock that belonged to my great-grandparents. It's probably more than seventy years old and sits in a wooden case on a shelf in my grandparents' living room, where it's stood for as long as I can remember. The clock needs to be wound by hand, and it makes this soft ticking sound that most people stop noticing after a few minutes. It's no longer perfectly accurate, but my grandfather still adjusts it every week, and that small ritual seems to give him real pleasure. The family keeps it because it survived several house moves and appears in many old photographs, so it's kind of a witness to our history. It's one of the few physical objects that connects different generations of our family, because everyone has grown up hearing that same tick. Replacing it with a modern clock would be easy, but accuracy isn't really its purpose. Its value comes from the memories and continuity it represents, and I've started asking my grandfather about the stories connected to it, because each answer adds another layer to an object I once barely noticed.", phrases: ["wound by hand", "connects different generations", "the continuity it represents"] },
  { id: "p2-31", topic: "A difficult decision", prompt: "Describe a difficult decision you made.", bullets: ["what the decision was", "when you made it", "what options you considered", "why it was difficult"], answer: "A really difficult decision I made was whether to leave a stable job and spend several months improving my English. The job gave me a regular income, but the opportunities for growth were limited, and I could feel myself learning nothing new by the end of each month. I thought about studying only in the evenings, but I knew progress would be slow because I was usually exhausted after work, and tired practice is rarely effective practice. The decision was hard because there was no guaranteed result, and I didn't want to burn through my savings on something that might fail. So I made a detailed budget, talked to people I trusted, and set a clear time limit for the study period, which gave the risk some boundaries. In the end, I chose to leave, and the first month was uncomfortable because no salary was coming in. But honestly, the months that followed were difficult yet productive, and they proved to me that a well-planned risk can open doors that safety never will. I've never regretted taking it. And honestly, that decision taught me more about myself than any course I took afterwards, because I learned that fear of failure is usually worse than the failure itself.", phrases: ["limited opportunities for growth", "no guaranteed result", "a responsible risk"] },
  { id: "p2-32", topic: "A challenge", prompt: "Describe a challenge you successfully completed.", bullets: ["what the challenge was", "why you did it", "how you completed it", "how you felt afterwards"], answer: "A challenge I completed was running a ten-kilometre charity race. Before I signed up, I rarely ran more than two kilometres, so the distance felt pretty intimidating, and I nearly backed out several times during the first week of training. I joined because a friend was raising money for a local children's programme, and I wanted to support both her and the cause. I followed an eight-week training plan and increased the distance gradually rather than trying to run fast, which protected me from injury and kept the sessions manageable. Some morning sessions were really tough, especially in bad weather, but recording every run helped me stay consistent, because I didn't want to break the chain on my own chart. On race day, I started slowly and finished without stopping, though those last two kilometres demanded more willpower than fitness. My time was average, but that honestly didn't matter. I felt proud because the result came from many ordinary training sessions rather than one burst of effort, and it changed my view of what I could achieve with a clear plan. The race also gave me a lasting habit, because I still run twice a week.", phrases: ["felt intimidating", "increased the distance gradually", "one burst of effort"] },
  { id: "p2-33", topic: "A success", prompt: "Describe something you did successfully.", bullets: ["what you did", "when you did it", "what made it difficult", "why you felt successful"], answer: "One thing I did successfully was give a presentation in English to an international group. I'd presented in my own language before, but speaking English in front of unfamiliar people made me nervous, and I could feel my heart racing every time I thought about it. So I prepared a simple structure, cut down the amount of text on each slide, and practiced aloud several times, because silent reading never transfers well to speaking. I also recorded myself to find the places where I spoke too quickly, and honestly, listening to my own voice was uncomfortable but extremely useful. During the presentation, I forgot one phrase, but I explained the idea in a different way and just kept going, which felt like a small victory at the time. The audience asked useful questions, and I could answer most of them clearly. I felt successful not because the presentation was perfect, but because I stayed calm when something went wrong and still got the main message across. Since then I've volunteered for two more presentations, and each one has felt slightly easier, which proves that confidence is built through repetition rather than talent.", phrases: ["practised aloud", "in a different way", "communicated the main message"] },
  { id: "p2-34", topic: "A mistake", prompt: "Describe a mistake that taught you something.", bullets: ["what the mistake was", "when it happened", "what the result was", "what you learned"], answer: "A mistake that taught me something happened when I booked a train ticket for the wrong date. I was planning a short business trip and made the booking quickly while answering messages, convinced I could do two things at once without any cost. I only noticed the problem at the station, when my ticket wouldn't work at the gate, and I just stood there reading the screen in disbelief. I had to buy a new ticket at a much higher price and arrived late for the first meeting, which made a bad situation even worse. I felt embarrassed because the mistake was completely avoidable, and I had no one to blame but my own carelessness. Since then, I've developed a simple habit: before paying for any travel booking, I read the route, date, and time aloud. It takes less than a minute but forces me to stop and check, and it's already saved me from repeating the same error. The experience taught me that speed isn't efficiency if careless work creates extra problems later, and now I treat every booking like a small ceremony, checking the date before sending important emails too.", phrases: ["completely avoidable", "developed a simple habit", "speed is not efficiency"] },
  { id: "p2-35", topic: "Helping someone", prompt: "Describe a time when you helped someone.", bullets: ["who you helped", "what the problem was", "what you did", "how you felt"], answer: "I once helped an older neighbour learn how to use a hospital booking app. She'd tried several times but found the registration steps confusing, and each failed attempt made her more reluctant to try again. So I sat with her, increased the text size on her phone, and explained each screen slowly, using her own appointments as examples rather than invented situations. Instead of completing the booking for her, I asked her to press the buttons herself and write down the main steps in her own words, because I knew doing it for her would only help once. A week later, she told me she'd booked another appointment without any help, and the pride in her voice was clear. I felt really pleased because the support gave her some independence rather than just solving one immediate problem. The experience also reminded me that digital systems which seem simple to frequent users can create real barriers for people who didn't grow up with them, and that patience is often the most useful form of help. I've actually helped another neighbour the same way since then, which makes me feel the lesson didn't stop with one person.", phrases: ["registration steps", "press the buttons herself", "create real barriers"] },
  { id: "p2-36", topic: "Being late", prompt: "Describe a time when you were late for something important.", bullets: ["what you were late for", "why you were late", "what happened", "what you learned"], answer: "I was once late for a job interview because the underground line stopped unexpectedly. I'd allowed twenty extra minutes, but the delay lasted almost forty, and every minute at the platform made my stomach tighten a little more. As soon as I realised the problem, I called the company, explained the situation, and gave them an updated arrival time, because staying silent would only have made things worse. I eventually arrived fifteen minutes late and apologised briefly without making a long excuse, which I think the interviewer appreciated. Fortunately, she was understanding, and the meeting went ahead, though I felt unsettled for the first ten minutes. I didn't get that particular job, though I don't know whether lateness affected the decision or whether another candidate was simply better. Since then, I check for transport problems before leaving and plan an alternative route for important appointments. The experience taught me that punctuality also means preparing for reasonable disruptions, not just arriving early when everything goes smoothly, and that communicating problems early is almost always the right move. I also learned that people usually respond better to bad news when they hear it early, because the interviewer later told me she appreciated the honest phone call more than the apology at the door.", phrases: ["stopped unexpectedly", "an updated arrival time", "reasonable disruptions"] },
  { id: "p2-37", topic: "Getting lost", prompt: "Describe a time when you got lost.", bullets: ["where you were", "how you got lost", "what you did", "how the experience ended"], answer: "I got lost while walking in an old neighbourhood in Kyoto. My phone battery was almost empty, and several narrow streets looked so similar that I couldn't tell whether I'd already walked down one of them. At first I kept walking because I assumed I'd recognise a landmark, but that only took me further away and made me more anxious. Eventually, I went into a small shop and showed the owner the address of my hotel, using my hands when my Japanese ran out. He couldn't speak much English, but he drew a simple map on a receipt and pointed out a temple I could use as a reference, and honestly, the drawing was surprisingly clear. I reached the hotel about forty minutes later, tired but relieved. The experience was a bit stressful, yet it also led me through quiet streets I'd never have visited deliberately, full of tiny gardens and washing lines. Now I carry a small power bank and save important addresses offline, and I actually laugh about the day whenever I remember it. In fact, I'd even recommend getting slightly lost in a new city once in a while, because the places you find by accident often stay with you the longest.", phrases: ["recognise a landmark", "use as a reference", "save important addresses offline"] },
  { id: "p2-38", topic: "Good news", prompt: "Describe some good news you received.", bullets: ["what the news was", "when you received it", "who told you", "why it mattered"], answer: "Some good news I received was that a close friend had been accepted into a postgraduate programme abroad. She sent me a voice message right after reading the email, and I could hear in her voice that she was both excited and shocked, speaking faster than she normally does. The news mattered because she'd spent nearly a year preparing her application while working full-time, often revising essays late at night after long shifts. There were several moments when she considered giving up, especially after one university rejected her, and I remember exactly how low she felt that week. I knew how much effort was behind the result, so her success felt genuinely meaningful, almost as if I'd shared the work. We celebrated with dinner that weekend and talked about practical plans for moving, from visas to furniture. The news also encouraged me, because it showed that a disappointing first result doesn't necessarily decide the final outcome, and that persistence can quietly pay off. Honestly, her example even pushed me to apply for something I'd been postponing, because I saw what happens when someone refuses to give up after a rejection.", phrases: ["both excited and shocked", "considered giving up", "the final outcome"] },
  { id: "p2-39", topic: "An interesting conversation", prompt: "Describe an interesting conversation you had.", bullets: ["who you spoke with", "where it happened", "what you discussed", "why it was interesting"], answer: "I had a really interesting conversation with a taxi driver during a long ride to the airport. We started by discussing traffic, which is usually where such conversations end, but then he told me he'd worked in three different cities over twenty years, and the topic completely changed. He described how each city had changed, why younger workers were moving further from the centre, and how the same streets look at night now compared with a decade ago. What made the conversation interesting was the combination of personal experience and social change, because he wasn't quoting statistics but describing things he'd actually seen. His observations weren't based on formal research, yet he'd noticed patterns through thousands of daily journeys and conversations with passengers, and his conclusions often matched what economists write about. I asked questions and mostly listened, which felt like the right thing to do. By the time we reached the airport, I felt I understood the city from a completely different angle. It reminded me that useful knowledge often appears in unexpected places, and honestly, that conversation changed how I travel, because now I talk to local drivers and shopkeepers instead of staring at my phone the whole way.", phrases: ["personal experience and social change", "noticed patterns", "from a different angle"] },
  { id: "p2-40", topic: "A complaint", prompt: "Describe a time when you complained about a service.", bullets: ["what the service was", "what went wrong", "how you complained", "what the result was"], answer: "I once complained to an online shop after receiving a damaged desk lamp. The outside box looked fine, but the lamp had a large crack across the base and wouldn't switch on, so it was completely useless. I took clear photos, found the order number, and contacted customer service through the app, describing the problem calmly rather than writing an angry message. The first automated response wasn't useful, so I asked for a human agent and briefly repeated the facts, which I've learned is more effective than starting the whole story over. Once the agent reviewed the photos, the company sent a new lamp and arranged free collection of the damaged one, so the process ended fairly. The result was satisfactory, though it took several messages over two days. The experience showed me that an effective complaint should be specific, supported by evidence, and clear about the solution you want, and I've used that method successfully several times since. I also learned that complaining doesn't have to be aggressive, because a polite but firm message usually produces a better result than an emotional one.", phrases: ["a large crack", "requested a human agent", "supported by evidence"] },
  { id: "p2-41", topic: "A memorable trip", prompt: "Describe a memorable journey you took.", bullets: ["where you went", "who you went with", "what happened", "why it was memorable"], answer: "A memorable journey I took was a three-day train trip with two friends to a mountain town. We chose the train instead of flying because we wanted to watch the landscape change along the way, from flat farmland to narrow valleys and pine forests. During the journey, heavy rain delayed us for several hours, and we arrived after dark, hungry and a bit frustrated. Our original plan for the first evening just disappeared, so we found a small noodle shop near the station and ended up spending the night talking with the owner and other travellers, who told us which trails were worth walking. The next morning, the rain stopped and the mountains were covered in mist, and the view from our window looked completely different from the evening before. The trip was memorable because the delay forced us to slow down and improvise, and honestly, we ended up preferring the unplanned evening to anything we'd scheduled. Some of the best moments were completely unplanned, which changed the way I organise later journeys, because now I always leave gaps in my plans. I still consider that trip one of the best I've taken.", phrases: ["along the way", "forced us to improvise", "completely unplanned"] },
  { id: "p2-42", topic: "A special meal", prompt: "Describe a meal you really enjoyed.", bullets: ["what the meal was", "where you ate it", "who you were with", "why it was enjoyable"], answer: "A meal I really enjoyed was a hotpot dinner with my university friends after we hadn't met for almost two years. We chose a small restaurant we used to visit as students, even though none of us lived nearby anymore, because the place carried too many memories to abandon. The food itself was familiar rather than luxurious: thin slices of meat, vegetables, tofu, and a spicy broth that gradually got stronger as the meal went on. What made the meal special was the conversation, because everyone's life had changed, but our old humour came back almost immediately. Since hotpot is shared and cooked slowly at the table, nobody rushed to leave, and the slow pace gave us time to catch up properly. We stayed for more than three hours, adding food and exchanging stories, and the owner even joined us for a toast when he recognised us. The dinner reminded me that a memorable meal depends as much on timing and company as it does on the quality of the dishes. We've already promised to repeat it every year, because that evening reminded us what the friendship is really made of.", phrases: ["familiar rather than luxurious", "returned almost immediately", "timing and company"] },
  { id: "p2-43", topic: "A celebration", prompt: "Describe a celebration you attended.", bullets: ["what was celebrated", "where it happened", "who attended", "what you enjoyed"], answer: "A celebration I attended was my cousin's wedding, which took place in a hotel near our hometown. Around a hundred relatives and friends came, including family members I hadn't seen for years, and the room felt full of overlapping conversations. The ceremony was simple, and the couple showed a short video about how they met, which made several older relatives wipe their eyes. What I enjoyed most was the relaxed atmosphere after dinner, because there was no strict schedule of speeches or games. Older relatives talked, children played, and different groups gradually mixed together, so the evening developed its own natural rhythm. I also helped guide guests to their tables, which made me feel involved rather than just an observer, and honestly, that's a much more satisfying way to attend a wedding. The wedding was memorable because it brought several generations together for a positive reason. Large family events can sometimes feel formal, but this one felt warm and genuinely personal. It also made me think about my own family traditions, because seeing everyone together reminded me how easily such gatherings become rare once people move away.", phrases: ["a relaxed atmosphere", "felt involved", "several generations"] },
  { id: "p2-44", topic: "A sports event", prompt: "Describe a sports event you watched.", bullets: ["what the event was", "where you watched it", "who you watched with", "why it was exciting"], answer: "A sports event I remember was an international badminton final that I watched at home with my dad. Neither of us follows every tournament, but the match involved two evenly matched players and went to a deciding game, so the result was impossible to predict. The rallies were incredibly fast, yet both players stayed patient and waited for the right moment to attack, which made the match feel like chess played at high speed. My dad and I started discussing tactics and predicting where each serve would go, and for an hour we forgot about everything else in the house. The final points were separated by only a tiny margin, so every mistake mattered, and we both stood up when the last rally began. It was exciting because the result stayed uncertain until the very end. Watching with someone else also made it more engaging, because we reacted together and kept talking about the match for days afterwards, replaying the key points from memory. We still argue about that last rally, and honestly, I suspect we always will. And honestly, that match also got me into watching badminton more carefully, because now I notice the tactical decisions behind every rally instead of just following the shuttle.", phrases: ["evenly matched players", "a deciding game", "a tiny margin"] },
  { id: "p2-45", topic: "Unusual weather", prompt: "Describe a time when the weather changed your plans.", bullets: ["what the weather was", "what you had planned", "what you did instead", "how you felt"], answer: "The weather changed my plans during a seaside trip when a strong storm arrived unexpectedly. My friends and I had planned to spend the afternoon walking along the coast, but the wind became dangerous and heavy rain started within minutes, so we ran back to the guesthouse completely soaked. We returned to our room and felt pretty disappointed at first, because the whole reason we'd chosen the seaside was the outdoor weather. But the owner suggested a covered local market, so we took a bus there instead, and the change of plan turned out to be a gift. We tried regional snacks, watched craftspeople at work, and bought ingredients to cook dinner together, things we'd never have chosen in fine weather. By evening, the storm was roaring outside while we were eating in the warm kitchen, which made the food taste even better. The day turned out to be memorable because we adapted rather than waiting for the original plan to become possible, and it taught me to leave some flexibility in any travel schedule. I also learned that a good travel companion is someone who can change plans without complaining.", phrases: ["arrived unexpectedly", "initially felt disappointed", "leave some flexibility"] },
  { id: "p2-46", topic: "A day off", prompt: "Describe an ideal day off.", bullets: ["when it would be", "where you would spend it", "what you would do", "why it would be ideal"], answer: "My ideal day off would be a cool, sunny weekday with no urgent messages, because the weekday quiet is part of what makes it special. I'd start without an alarm, make some coffee, and read for an hour, letting the morning begin slowly instead of jumping straight into tasks. Late in the morning, I'd take a long walk in a park or along the river, then have lunch at a small restaurant I'd never tried before, choosing something I wouldn't normally order. I'd avoid any detailed schedule and keep my phone on silent, because checking messages would quietly turn the day back into work. In the afternoon, I might visit a bookshop or watch a film at home, and in the evening I'd cook something simple and eat without a screen. The day would be ideal because it combines gentle activity with genuine rest, which is a balance most busy weeks never allow. I don't need an expensive trip; I mainly want freedom from deadlines and enough time to notice what I'm doing instead of rushing through it. Honestly, I've started protecting one such day each month, because rest doesn't happen by itself unless you deliberately plan it.", phrases: ["no urgent messages", "avoid a detailed schedule", "freedom from deadlines"] },
  { id: "p2-47", topic: "Learning something new", prompt: "Describe something new you learned recently.", bullets: ["what you learned", "why you learned it", "how you learned it", "how you felt"], answer: "Recently, I learned how to make a simple personal budget in a spreadsheet. I wanted to understand where my money was going rather than relying on a rough feeling, because I had no real answer when someone asked how much I spent on food each month. I watched two short tutorials and created categories for fixed costs, daily spending, and savings, and honestly, the first version took me an entire evening to build. At first the formulas looked complicated, but I started with basic totals and added features gradually, which made the learning process far less frightening. After tracking expenses for a month, I noticed that small convenience purchases added up to more than I expected, and I could actually see the pattern clearly. I felt more informed rather than restricted, because I could consciously decide what was worth spending on instead of discovering the damage later. The skill is pretty basic, but it's made my financial decisions clearer and given me a useful sense of control over my own life. I'd recommend the exercise to anyone who feels anxious about money, because seeing the numbers clearly is far less scary than guessing in the dark.", phrases: ["relying on a rough feeling", "added features gradually", "rather than restricted"] },
  { id: "p2-48", topic: "A presentation", prompt: "Describe a presentation you gave.", bullets: ["what it was about", "who you presented to", "how you prepared", "how it went"], answer: "I gave a presentation about reducing food waste to a group of colleagues. I had ten minutes, so the main challenge was choosing a few useful points instead of including every statistic I found, which was actually harder than writing a longer talk. I organised the talk around one problem, three practical actions, and a short example from our office, so the audience could leave with something they could actually do. I used simple slides with photographs and rehearsed with a timer, because I've learned that timing always feels different when you say the words aloud. During the presentation, I spoke a bit faster than planned, but I finished on time and the audience asked several practical questions, which is a good sign for any talk. Afterwards, our team introduced a shared shelf for unopened food, a tiny change that came directly from my presentation. The presentation went well because it led to one small action, not because my delivery was perfect, and that result made the preparation feel worthwhile. Honestly, I now measure any talk by the action it creates, because a presentation that changes one small behaviour is more valuable than applause.", phrases: ["selecting a few useful points", "rehearsed with a timer", "led to one small action"] },
  { id: "p2-49", topic: "An important rule", prompt: "Describe a rule that you think is important.", bullets: ["what the rule is", "where it applies", "why it exists", "why you think it matters"], answer: "An important rule is that drivers must stop for pedestrians at marked crossings. The rule applies on city streets and exists because pedestrians are much more vulnerable than people inside vehicles, and a mistake that merely inconveniences a driver can seriously injure a walker. I think it matters because a crossing is only useful when people can trust that drivers will respect it, and trust is built through consistent enforcement. When the rule is ignored, children, older people, and anyone moving slowly face particular danger, because they can't quickly get out of the way. Good enforcement is necessary, but road design and public education also play a role, since a well-designed crossing can make compliance almost automatic. Drivers may lose only a few seconds by stopping, while failing to stop can cause permanent harm, so the cost of obeying the rule is incredibly small. This is a simple example of a rule that limits individual convenience slightly to protect everyone using a shared public space, and honestly, I believe small acts of respect, repeated by thousands of people, are what make a city safe and pleasant.", phrases: ["much more vulnerable", "good enforcement", "shared public space"] },
  { id: "p2-50", topic: "An environmental activity", prompt: "Describe an activity you did to help the environment.", bullets: ["what the activity was", "when and where you did it", "who joined you", "what effect it had"], answer: "I joined a neighbourhood clean-up beside a river last spring. About twenty volunteers met on a Saturday morning, and the organisers provided gloves, bags, and separate containers for recyclable material, so we could sort the waste as we collected it. We worked for two hours and collected plastic bottles, food packaging, and a few abandoned household items, and by the end the bags formed a small mountain by the path. I joined with a friend, which made the repetitive work more enjoyable, because we could chat while bending down for bottles. The area looked noticeably better afterwards, though I understood that one clean-up couldn't solve the larger problem of waste. Its main effect was to make the issue visible, because people walking past asked questions, and several said they wanted to join the next event. The activity also made me more careful about using disposable items, since I'd seen with my own eyes where they can eventually end up, and that personal memory is stronger than any poster. I've since attended two more clean-ups, and each time the number of volunteers has grown, which shows that small local actions can slowly change how a community behaves.", phrases: ["separate containers", "made the issue visible", "where they can eventually end up"] },
];

